@@ -38,7 +38,7 @@ Legacy Hayai and current J2K both called their database `tachiyomi.db`, but thei
 
 On first database open, an idempotent importer validates the legacy schema and requires the fresh J2K target to be pristine. It imports compatible J2K core rows in dependency order, imports quotes/novel/E-Hentai/search data into typed tables, and also archives every source row with table and row identity in `hayai_legacy_rows`. The raw archive preserves columns J2K cannot represent. Inserts abort on any identity conflict; a non-pristine target is refused rather than silently merged or overwritten. The importer checks foreign keys and records a durable result. The source database is never modified. A failed import rolls back and records a recoverable diagnostic without entering an app-start retry loop. A separate idempotent quote-recovery generation invalidates the superseded quote markers, replays archived `series_quotes` payloads, then restores pre-SQLite quote files from the retained `__APP_STATE_storage_dir` or the legacy `Hayai/quotes/novel_<id>.json` default. It ports both exact shapes from legacy Hayai commit `c1aab40f664dd2eec09774b653535021302f8e86`, never deletes source rows or files, never overwrites an existing typed quote, and does not record completion while storage is inaccessible, a file is unreadable, or a manga identity is absent. Because modern scoped-storage devices can deny the obsolete raw path even when the files remain visible to the system picker, the Saved quotes UI also accepts multiple JSON documents through Android's Storage Access Framework and merges every resolvable novel in one transaction. Manual recovery prefers the preserved numeric ID and falls back only to one exact, case-insensitive novel-title match; absent or ambiguous identities remain rejected.
 
-J2K's existing source-migration screens remain the only migration surface. Their source rows are partitioned by the shared Manga/Novel policy, and the target chooser accepts every discoverable `CatalogueSource`, including local and JavaScript novel sources. One adapter call invokes Hayai's `NovelMigrationDataMover` inside J2K's existing transaction after target chapters exist; the mover does not open a nested transaction. Before writing, it builds a one-to-one chapter-number map for every chapter carrying selected J2K chapter state or Hayai statistics/highlights and refuses missing or ambiguous mappings. The existing Chapters flag continues to govern J2K read, bookmark, history, `last_page_read`, and `pages_left` state. Hayai quotes, word counts, and highlights always transfer with the novel. Replace preserves row IDs, rebinds quote/highlight ownership, and either rebinds a statistic to the target chapter or removes its source row when the target already has the same count. Copy retains every source row, inserts quotes/highlights under deterministic destination IDs, and inserts a target statistic only when one is absent. An exact pre-existing deterministic copy is an idempotent no-op; a different row at that ID advances through a bounded deterministic identity sequence and aborts if no identity is available. Any different target statistic aborts the caller's transaction, and migration never uses conflict-ignore. Target-owned rows remain intact. Source metadata, repositories, plugin identity, the legacy archive, and offline payloads remain under their original ownership and are not relabelled during migration.
+J2K's existing source-migration screens remain the only migration surface. Their source rows are partitioned by the shared Manga/Novel policy, and the target chooser accepts every discoverable `CatalogueSource`, including local and JavaScript novel sources. The policy does not demote an imported novel when its old source is unavailable: a live novel capability, an exact legacy novel genre, a remembered novel-plugin source ID, or retained quotes, highlights, or chapter statistics is sufficient proof. One adapter call invokes Hayai's `NovelMigrationDataMover` inside J2K's existing transaction after target chapters exist; the mover does not open a nested transaction. Before writing, it builds a one-to-one chapter-number map for every chapter carrying selected J2K chapter state or Hayai statistics/highlights and refuses missing or ambiguous mappings. The existing Chapters flag continues to govern J2K read, bookmark, history, `last_page_read`, and `pages_left` state. Hayai quotes, word counts, and highlights always transfer with the novel. Replace preserves row IDs, rebinds quote/highlight ownership, and either rebinds a statistic to the target chapter or removes its source row when the target already has the same count. Copy retains every source row, inserts quotes/highlights under deterministic destination IDs, and inserts a target statistic only when one is absent. An exact pre-existing deterministic copy is an idempotent no-op; a different row at that ID advances through a bounded deterministic identity sequence and aborts if no identity is available. Any different target statistic aborts the caller's transaction, and migration never uses conflict-ignore. Target-owned rows remain intact. Source metadata, repositories, plugin identity, the legacy archive, and offline payloads remain under their original ownership and are not relabelled during migration.
 
